## Data

The Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm (AAA) patients' dataset has 6,000 records, produced by Vascular Governance North West of England and covering 1997 to 2012.

## Goal

Use data mining to discover the relationships between patients' medical records and how long they stay in hospital (length of stay, or LOS).

## Approach

Medical data is sensitive, and this dataset contained noise, outliers, and missing values, so the project started with a careful preprocessing stage to produce a clean, valid dataset.

Four model learning methods were then applied, and their accuracy and validity assessed:

- Decision Tree (C4.5)
- Rule Induction
- k-nearest neighbours (KNN)
- Naive Bayes

## Results

Thanks to the preprocessing, some of the models reached high accuracy, between 70 and 84 percent. Merging the results of the decision tree and rule induction models gave a set of meaningful rules that could help medical managers manage their resources better.

This was my undergraduate final project, supervised by Professor Mo Saraee, with Dr. Abdolreza Mirzaei as referee. It was graded 20 out of 20.
